    "overview_kicker": "Overall effect",
    "overview_title": "A plant room works as a system",
    "overview_copy": "The effect does not come from using as many individual products as possible, but from coordinated layers: large plants as anchors, calmer planters, structured substrates, deliberately placed light and smaller botanical scenes.",
    "overview_point_1": "Statement plants define sight lines.",
    "overview_point_2": "Planters repeat material and form.",
    "overview_point_3": "Lighting connects plant care with atmosphere.",
    "overview_cta": "Explore Interior Solutions",
    "overview_secondary": "Configure plant lighting",
    "prev": "← Back",
    "next": "Next →",
    "end": "End",
    "tabs_aria": "Interior areas",
    "content_eyebrow": "WHY THIS SOLUTION WORKS",
    "content_heading": "One room, several botanical layers",
    "card_plants_title": "Large plants create structure",
    "card_plants_text": "Monstera, Strelitzia and similar specimen plants work as visual anchors. They are placed deliberately along sight lines and transitions instead of being distributed evenly.",
    "card_substrate_title": "Substrate remains part of the design",
    "card_substrate_text": "The technical layer does not disappear: root space, drainage, air pores and water retention determine which planter size and which mix make sense.",
    "card_light_title": "Lighting becomes an interior element",
    "card_light_text": "Plant lighting does not have to look technical. Pendant, spot or shelf lighting can be planned to serve the plants while supporting the room's visual effect.",
    "card_scenes_title": "Small scenes create depth",
    "card_scenes_text": "Terrariums, succulents and vertical planting complement the large plants. The result is a layered plant environment rather than a collection of separate pots.",
    "pathways_aria": "Matching configurators and collections",
    "path_interior_title": "Interior Solutions",
    "path_interior_text": "Explore room planting, plants, planters, light and terrariums as one connected solution.",
    "path_substrate_title": "Substrate configurator",
    "path_substrate_text": "Build a custom substrate mix matched to the plant.",
    "path_light_title": "Grow-light configurator",
    "path_light_text": "Plan lighting around location, plant needs and room design.",
    "path_plants_title": "Houseplants",
    "path_plants_text": "Choose and combine plants for interior planting.",
    "path_planters_title": "Planters & pots",
    "path_planters_text": "Find planters that suit plant size, root space and style.",
    "path_succulents_title": "Succulent world",
    "path_succulents_text": "Explore succulents and terrarium ideas as a compact plant world.",
    "related_aria": "Related LEAFerservice areas",
    "related_interior": "Explore Interior Solutions",
    "related_all": "Explore the range",
    "plants_kicker": "Statement plants",
    "plants_title": "Large leaves give the room its architecture",
    "plants_copy": "The Monstera works almost like a piece of furniture here: large enough to act as a visual anchor, with enough free space around the leaves. Supporting plants repeat the green effect without competing with the main subject.",
    "plants_point_1": "One main subject instead of many equally large plants.",
    "plants_point_2": "Layered heights create depth.",
    "plants_point_3": "Free space around large leaves keeps the composition calm.",
    "plants_cta": "Choose houseplants",
    "plants_secondary": "Connect with room planning",
    "planter_kicker": "Planter & substrate",
    "planter_title": "Function and design need to work together",
    "planter_copy": "Large planters give roots reserve and stabilise the proportions in the room. Substrate is selected for the plant and watering method; mineral and coarse components can deliberately influence aeration and drainage.",
    "planter_point_1": "Planter size follows root space and plant size.",
    "planter_point_2": "Material and colour repeat room surfaces.",
    "planter_point_3": "Substrate is planned for function rather than decoration.",
    "planter_cta": "Configure substrate",
    "planter_secondary": "Choose planters",
    "light_kicker": "Plant lighting",
    "light_title": "Grow lights can look like interior lighting",
    "light_copy": "The lighting is not placed beside the furniture as visible equipment; it is integrated into pendant, spot or shelf solutions. Position, distance and the plant's light requirement are decisive.",
    "light_point_1": "Integrate the light source into existing visual axes where possible.",
    "light_point_2": "Match distance and beam angle to the plant.",
    "light_point_3": "Combine a warm interior feel with useful plant light.",
    "light_cta": "Configure plant lighting",
    "light_secondary": "View Interior Solutions",
    "terrarium_kicker": "Terrarium",
    "terrarium_title": "A small plant world as its own focal point",
    "terrarium_copy": "The terrarium creates a compact second layer in the room. Succulents, mineral structure and glass deliberately contrast with the large specimen plants and create a concentrated detail point.",
    "terrarium_point_1": "Smaller scales complement large plants.",
    "terrarium_point_2": "Glass adds another material layer.",
    "terrarium_point_3": "Plan substrate structure and plant choice together.",
    "terrarium_cta": "Explore the succulent world",
    "terrarium_secondary": "View terrarium solutions",
    "vertical_kicker": "Vertical planting",
    "vertical_title": "Use height without losing floor space",
    "vertical_copy": "Shelves and upper levels carry smaller plants and hanging growth. Greenery moves upward, making the room feel more densely planted without filling the floor and walkways.",
    "vertical_point_1": "Vertical levels free up the floor.",
    "vertical_point_2": "Hanging plants connect furniture and architecture.",
    "vertical_point_3": "Keep care access despite denser planting.",
    "vertical_cta": "Supports & vertical greenery",
    "vertical_secondary": "View Interior Solutions",
    "breadcrumb_guides": "Guides",
    "seo_keywords": "plant interiors, interior planting, houseplants, plant substrate, planters, grow lights, succulent terrarium, vertical planting",
    "faq_q1": "How do I plan plants sensibly in a room?",
    "faq_a1": "Start with location, light and available space. Then plan plant height, growth habit, planter, substrate and additional lighting as one connected system.",
    "faq_q2": "Which substrate works for a plant interior?",
    "faq_a2": "The substrate depends on the individual plant and watering method. Aeration, water retention and drainage can be adjusted through suitable mixes and components.",
    "faq_q3": "How can I integrate grow lighting discreetly into the room?",
    "faq_a3": "Plant lighting can be incorporated as pendant, spot or shelf lighting along existing lighting axes. Light requirements, distance and beam angle remain important.",
    "faq_q4": "Can a succulent terrarium be part of the room planting?",
    "faq_a4": "Yes. A terrarium adds a smaller botanical layer to large houseplants and can act as its own focal point within the plant interior."
